.TH SPARKCTL 1 "November 2026" "sparkctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sparkctl \- manage Spark Swapper, Sparkia, Docker and the services on a DGX Spark
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sparkctl
.br
.B sparkctl
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments,
.B sparkctl
opens a full-screen terminal interface with six screens:
.TP
.B 1 Overview
memory, disks, load, the GPU, what Spark Swapper serves, the Sparkia stack, Docker and any failed service.
.TP
.B 2 Swapper
the models: swap, install (including a model's other builds), change a model's context window and options,
follow a job's log, cancel it, run the speed check, turn pictures on or off, delete a model's files, update the Swapper.
.TP
.B 3 Sparkia
the Docker Compose stack in /opt/sparkia: each service's log, restart, stop, start, recreate, up, pull, down;
pin the release (SPARKIA_TAG), run the updater and follow it, the firewall's status and live traffic, a shell in a container.
.TP
.B 4 Docker
containers (with CPU and memory), images, volumes and disk usage: logs, start, stop, restart, kill, remove, a shell,
inspect, pull, prune.
.TP
.B 5 Services
the systemd units the Swapper and Sparkia run on: start, stop, restart, enable or disable at boot, clear a failure,
status and the journal.
.TP
.B 6 Run
saved command lines and any command you type, run in the terminal; and the history of what sparkctl did, with its output.
.PP
Every action that changes something says the command it runs and asks first when it can't be undone. Root actions go
through sudo: when sudo needs a password, sparkctl hands the terminal to sudo once (press
.B S
to do it ahead). Logs follow live and can be searched, filtered and saved. Press
.B ?
for the keys.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR status " [" \-\-json ]
What's serving, Sparkia, Docker and the services, in one look.
.TP
.B models
The Swapper's models and their state.
.TP
.BI swap " MODEL"
Swap Spark Swapper to MODEL (its key, its title or a unique part of either) and follow the swap.
.TP
.BR login " [" \fIUSER\fR ]
Sign in to Spark Swapper. The session and the fingerprint of the Swapper's certificate are kept in
~/.config/sparkctl/state.json, readable only by you.
.TP
.B logout
Sign out.
.TP
.BI logs " TARGET"
Follow a log: \fBswapper\fR, \fBupdater\fR, \fBsparkia\fR[\fB:\fISERVICE\fR], \fBdocker:\fICONTAINER\fR, \fBunit:\fINAME\fR.
.TP
.B doctor
Check everything sparkctl relies on and say what's wrong (exit status 1 when something is).
.TP
.B version
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sparkctl/config.json
The machine's settings: the Swapper's address, the Sparkia folder, the services shown, saved commands.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sparkctl/config.json
Your own settings, over the machine's.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sparkctl/state.json
Your Swapper sign-in and the certificate you trusted.
.TP
.I ~/sparkctl-logs/
Logs saved from a log view.
.SH SECURITY
Spark Swapper's certificate is self-signed: sparkctl trusts the one it sees first and refuses a different one until you
press \fBT\fR on the Swapper screen. Passwords are never stored; sudo asks for its own.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR docker (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
